---
import InsightArticle from '../../layouts/InsightArticle.astro';
import StudioNote from '../../components/insights/StudioNote.astro';
import { article } from '../../data/studio/what-a-content-credential-records';
const rawBase = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const base = rawBase.endsWith('/') ? rawBase : `${rawBase}/`;

// Page addresses used in the tables. The specification anchors were checked against the page source on 14 November 2026.
const spec = 'https://spec.c2pa.org/specifications/specifications/2.5/specs/C2PA_Specification.html';
const explainer = 'https://spec.c2pa.org/specifications/specifications/2.2/explainer/Explainer.html';
const faq = 'https://c2pa.org/faqs/';
const caiStart = 'https://opensource.contentauthenticity.org/docs/getting-started';
const caiFaq = 'https://opensource.contentauthenticity.org/docs/getting-started/faqs';

// Table 1: the parts of a credential. Column two is the specification's own term; column three is our paraphrase.
const parts = [
  ['What a tool says about the file', 'Assertion', 'A statement made by the signer or gathered when the claim was made, for example camera details, actions taken on the file or a thumbnail', `${spec}#_assertion`, 'Spec 2.5, Assertion'],
  ['The signed list', 'Claim', 'A signed, tamper-evident structure that points to a set of assertions and holds what is needed to bind them to the file; it also names the claim generator that produced it', `${spec}#_claim`, 'Spec 2.5, Claim'],
  ['The signature', 'Claim signature', 'A digital signature on the claim, made with the signer\'s private key', `${spec}#_glossary`, 'Spec 2.5, Glossary'],
  ['The whole bundle', 'C2PA Manifest (plain-language term: Content Credential)', 'The assertions, one claim and the claim signature together', `${spec}#_content_credential`, 'Spec 2.5, Content Credential'],
  ['The link to the file', 'Hard binding', 'One or more hashes that identify the whole file or a part of it, so a validator can check that the manifest belongs to this file and that the file is unmodified', `${spec}#_binding_to_content`, 'Spec 2.5, Binding to Content'],
  ['A backup way to match', 'Soft binding', 'A fingerprint or an invisible watermark in the content, used to find a manifest again after it has been removed', `${spec}#_soft_binding`, 'Spec 2.5, Soft binding'],
  ['What was done', 'Actions assertion, with a digital source type', 'The edits and other actions taken on the file and, for a new file, how it began, such as a digital capture or trained algorithmic media', `${spec}#_actions`, 'Spec 2.5, Actions'],
  ['What went in', 'Ingredient', 'An asset used to build or edit another, or an input to a process such as an AI model; it can carry its own credential', `${spec}#ingredient_assertion`, 'Spec 2.5, Ingredient'],
  ['What it looked like', 'Thumbnail', 'An approximate visual representation of the asset when the claim was made', `${spec}#_thumbnails`, 'Spec 2.5, Thumbnails'],
  ['Details about an AI model', 'AI Disclosure assertion', 'A machine-readable assertion about the model used. If it is present it must give a model type; a model name, an identifier and a human oversight level are optional fields. Added in version 2.4', `${spec}#_ai_disclosure`, 'Spec 2.5, AI Disclosure'],
];

// Table 2: readings a valid credential does not back up. Links are [label, url] pairs.
const readings = [
  ['The scene in the picture is true', 'The CAI says validation gives evidence of integrity and, for a trusted signer, of where the signature came from. It does not prove that the depicted content, or every assertion, is true or complete.', [['CAI, Getting started', caiStart]]],
  ['Passing validation means the signer is trusted', 'The spec treats valid and trusted as different states, and trusted adds a check of the signing credential. The CAI adds that a valid certificate chain alone does not establish content integrity or factual truth.', [['Spec 2.5, Manifest states', `${spec}#_manifest_states`], ['CAI, Getting started', caiStart]]],
  ['The signer made the image', 'The CAI says the signer\'s identity is not necessarily the creator\'s, and a certificate may name an application, device or organisation. The C2PA FAQ says the core specification does not support attributing content to individuals or organisations, and that extensions are being developed.', [['CAI, Getting started', caiStart], ['C2PA FAQ', faq]]],
  ['The name a viewer shows owns the rights', 'The CAI FAQ says that in many cases the name shown by viewers such as its Inspect tool is the name of the exporter, not the rights owner.', [['CAI FAQ', caiFaq]]],
  ['The time shown is when it was made', 'The spec says ordinary time values record the signer\'s claim, and a claimed time of signing is not independently authoritative. A trusted time stamp from a time-stamping authority is separate evidence.', [['Spec 2.5, Time in assertions', `${spec}#_specifications_of_time_in_assertions`]]],
  ['The edit history is complete', 'The spec says that when the all-actions flag is false, or left out, unrecorded actions may have been performed. The C2PA explainer says provenance is not always complete.', [['Spec 2.5, Actions', `${spec}#_actions`], ['C2PA explainer 2.2', explainer]]],
  ['The metadata, such as location, was vouched for', 'The spec says only signed created assertions are attributed to the signer. Other metadata in the file, such as EXIF, is not attributed to the signer even when the hash covers it, and the signer is not responsible for the accuracy of a GPS position there.', [['Spec 2.5, Asset metadata', `${spec}#asset_metadata_bindings`]]],
  ['A file with no credential is fake', 'The CAI says missing credentials do not by themselves indicate false content, and that credentials give a positive signal about origin and history, not a negative one. The C2PA explainer says provenance is optional, and no assumption about trustworthiness should rest purely on whether a file uses credentials.', [['CAI, Getting started', caiStart], ['CAI FAQ', caiFaq], ['C2PA explainer 2.2', explainer]]],
];
---
<InsightArticle title={article.title} description={article.description} date={article.date} type={article.type} readTime={article.readTime} snapshot="2026-11-14" standfirst="A Content Credential is a signed record of what a tool says happened to a file, tied to that file by a hash, and the C2PA itself says it cannot show the content is true.">
  <p>A Content Credential is a signed bundle of statements about a file, tied to that file's contents by a cryptographic hash. The C2PA, the coalition that writes the standard, is open about the limit: its explainer says provenance information alone <a href={explainer} rel="noopener">"cannot tell you whether the digital content is true, accurate or factual"</a>.</p>
  <p>This piece covers what is inside that bundle, what happens when the file is changed or its metadata is stripped, and what a credential on an AI image could and could not show. We read the pages named in the tables on 14 November 2026. We tested no file, viewer or image generator, so nothing here says which AI tools write credentials or what a particular tool puts in them.</p>

  <div class="stat-row">
    <div class="stat"><b>2.5</b><span>newest specification version we found, dated October 2026</span></div>
    <div class="stat"><b>3</b><span>nested states a validator can report for a manifest: well-formed, valid, trusted</span></div>
    <div class="stat"><b>1</b><span>most hard bindings a single manifest may hold</span></div>
  </div>

  <h2>Version 2.5 of the C2PA specification is dated October 2026</h2>
  <p>The C2PA's <a href="https://c2pa.org/about/" rel="noopener">about page</a> describes it as a project of the Joint Development Foundation, a Washington-based non-profit. Its old specifications address now redirects to version 2.5, and the version history inside that document dates 2.5 to October 2026, with 2.4 in April 2026 and 2.3 in December 2025. The Content Authenticity Initiative (CAI) is a separate body: <a href="https://opensource.contentauthenticity.org/docs/introduction" rel="noopener">its documentation</a> calls it an Adobe-led, cross-industry consortium that promotes Content Credentials and maintains open-source software built on the C2PA specifications, and lists contentcredentials.org as the C2PA's consumer-friendly site.</p>
  <p>The download menu on the specification site still lists 2.3 and 2.2 files, and the address where a 2.5 explainer would sit returned a not-found page. We therefore cite the 2.2 explainer wherever we use an explainer and the 2.5 specification for everything else. The FAQ pages carry no version or date, so a detail in them may have changed since they were written.</p>

  <h2>A credential is a signed list of statements tied to one file</h2>
  <p>The specification builds a credential in layers. The smallest piece is an assertion, a statement either made by the signer or gathered when the claim was generated. Its own examples are camera details, actions such as clipping or colour correction, a thumbnail and the hashes that tie the record to the file. A claim collects references to the assertions and the information needed to bind them to the content, and it is digitally signed. The claim generator, which the spec defines as the hardware or software that produces the claim, signs on behalf of the signer, and the spec lists cameras, editing software and AI asset creators among its examples of claim generators.</p>
  <p>Assertions, the claim and the claim signature together make a manifest, and the spec calls Content Credential the preferred non-technical term for one. A file can carry several manifests in a manifest store. The last one is the active manifest, the one whose bindings a validator, meaning software that checks a credential, can test against the file. The table lists the parts a reader meets first.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table>
    <caption>Source: C2PA Technical Specification 2.5, read 14 November 2026. The second column is the specification's own term; the third is our paraphrase.</caption>
    <thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>What the spec calls it</th><th>What it records</th><th>Page</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {parts.map(([el, term, rec, url, label]) => <tr><td>{el}</td><td>{term}</td><td>{rec}</td><td><a href={url} rel="noopener">{label}</a></td></tr>)}
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>What goes into a given credential is the tool's choice. The CAI's FAQ says the information embedded depends on each implementer, and the specification only strongly encourages implementers to let creators and publishers control whether certain provenance data is included. Two credentials from two tools can therefore hold quite different amounts of detail.</p>

  <h2>Editing a file breaks its match with the credential</h2>
  <p>The hard binding is what ties a credential to one file. Per the spec, it lets a validator confirm that the manifest belongs with this file and that the file has not been modified, using values that can match only this file and no other. The hash covers the file's data apart from any byte ranges the claim generator excludes, and the spec says such ranges may hold only the manifest itself or file metadata such as EXIF and IPTC fields.</p>
  <p>When the hashes no longer match, the spec says the validator must reject the manifest and report a mismatch code, such as assertion.dataHash.mismatch for a credential that uses a data hash. It adds that the validator cannot tell whether the file was changed or the wrong manifest was found. The <a href="https://opensource.contentauthenticity.org/docs/manifest/reading/manifest-validation" rel="noopener">CAI's validation page</a> lists bits edited after signing as one cause of a validation error, next to a missing or tampered claim and an invalid signing credential.</p>
  <p>The spec says a hard binding matches only this asset, not other assets derived from it and not renditions produced from it, and it defines a rendition as a copy with a non-editorial change such as re-encoding or scaling. Taken together, a resized or re-encoded copy of an image is not expected to match the original's hard binding. That is our reading of two definitions; none of the pages we read works through a resized image as an example.</p>
  <p>Tools that support the standard handle edits differently from tools that do not. The CAI says an editing tool that supports it writes a new signed manifest for its output and lists the source file as an ingredient, rather than rewriting the original claim. A gap is possible too. The C2PA explainer says a file cropped in a tool without support may get no provenance update, and if it later goes into a supporting tool, the new signer implicitly attests to the earlier crop as well. The spec gives the actions list a flag for this: when it is false, or left out, unrecorded actions may have been performed.</p>

  <h2>Stripped credentials can sometimes be rediscovered from a stored copy</h2>
  <p>The C2PA FAQ says manifests are typically embedded in the file but can be separated from it. Its answer is Durable Content Credentials, which pair the manifest with a soft binding, meaning an invisible watermark or a fingerprint of the content, that can help rediscover the credential after it has been removed. The <a href="https://opensource.contentauthenticity.org/docs/durable-cr/" rel="noopener">CAI's page on the idea</a> adds the condition: a platform may remove manifest data, for example when its software does not support the standard, and a watermark or fingerprint can then be used to find the manifest again if a copy is stored in an online database. The CAI also says a rediscovered manifest still has to be checked for its signature and bindings.</p>
  <p>A screenshot is a separate case. The CAI's FAQ says credentials do not stop anyone taking a screenshot of an image, and that a screenshot would not include the original's C2PA metadata.</p>

  <h2>Eight readings the sources do not support</h2>
  <p>A credential that passes validation can show three nested things. The spec calls a manifest well-formed when it follows the specification's rules. It is valid when it is also unmodified since signing, has a good signature and a signing credential not shown as revoked, and it is trusted when its signing credential also receives the trusted result. If the parts of a file covered by the bindings have not changed and the active manifest is valid or trusted, the spec calls the file itself a valid asset.</p>
  <p>None of those states is a judgement about the picture. The specification quotes the C2PA's guiding principles as saying its specifications do not judge whether provenance data is good or bad, only whether the assertions can be validated as associated with the asset, are correctly formed and are free from tampering.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table>
    <caption>Sources: C2PA Technical Specification 2.5, C2PA explainer 2.2, C2PA FAQ and CAI documentation, all read 14 November 2026. The middle column is our paraphrase.</caption>
    <thead><tr><th>A reader might assume</th><th>What the pages say</th><th>Page</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {readings.map(([r, said, links]) => <tr><td>{r}</td><td>{said}</td><td>{links.map(([label, url], i) => <span><a href={url} rel="noopener">{label}</a>{i < links.length - 1 ? '; ' : ''}</span>)}</td></tr>)}
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h2>What a credential on an AI image could and could not show</h2>
  <p>If an image generator writes a manifest, the spec's rules for new files apply. A file created from scratch, which the spec says includes media generated by a generative AI model, must start its actions list with a c2pa.created action, and that action must carry a digital source type saying how the file began. The spec's own example for an image from a generative model uses the IPTC value trainedAlgorithmicMedia, and it says ingredients may be added to record the inputs that led to the output. The AI Disclosure assertion, added in version 2.4, can add the model type and, optionally, a model name, an identifier and a human oversight level of fully_autonomous, prompt_guided or human_validated. All of it is what the tool states and the signer vouches for, and the tool decides whether to include it.</p>
  <p>What a viewer shows depends on the viewer. The CAI describes its <a href="https://opensource.contentauthenticity.org/docs/getting-started/inspect" rel="noopener">Inspect tool</a> as a conforming validator product. For a file with a manifest it shows the asset's title from the active manifest, which may differ from the file name, and the name of whoever signed. It flags a signer that is not on the C2PA trust list as unrecognised, and for a file with no manifest store it reports that there is no Content Credential. The C2PA's own Verify page at contentcredentials.org needs JavaScript, so we could not read its text and did not run it.</p>
  <p>Three points from the CAI's FAQ matter most to someone posting AI images. The FAQ says that if an AI tool supports Content Credentials, they indicate whether an image was generated with AI, which makes the record depend on the tool. A screenshot of a credentialed image would carry none of the original's credential data. And a C2PA-enabled camera that photographs an AI image signs its own photo: the camera cannot analyse what it is photographing, so that credential does not say the picture was AI-generated.</p>

  <StudioNote article={article} snapshot="14 November 2026" />
</InsightArticle>
